WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.530
Write twelve as two times two times three.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.630
Write eighteen as two times three times three.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.270
Group copies of the same prime vertically.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.690
One column holds the twos.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.690
Another holds the threes.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.300
We can see exactly how many of each prime

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.530
each number uses.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.830
We can solve three problems using these counts.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.100
To divide into both numbers, a factor needs to fit

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.270
inside both rows.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.610
A common multiple has to contain both rows.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.720
For a square, the tiles must split into

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.890
two matching sets.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.640
Find the largest positive number that divides both

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.670
twelve and eighteen.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.620
Then find the smallest positive number that is a

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:41.590
multiple of both.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.160
Use the tiles, and decide how many copies of each

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.090
prime you need.

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.450
A common factor can use only the copies both

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:53.560
numbers can supply.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.130
Eighteen can supply one two and twelve can

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.860
supply one three.

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:01.460
Those are the smaller counts in the two columns.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.580
The highest common factor is two times three,

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:05.480
which is six.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.830
A common multiple needs enough copies to

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:09.770
cover either number.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.670
Twelve needs two twos.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.330
Eighteen needs two threes.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.770
Take the larger count in each column.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:19.210
Two twos and two threes multiply to thirty-six.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.560
It is the lowest common multiple.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:25.790
For each prime, the smaller count plus the larger count

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.480
equals the original two counts added together.

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.730
So the factors of six and thirty-six together use

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.050
exactly the same prime copies as twelve

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.220
and eighteen together.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:37.080
Both products are two hundred and sixteen.

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:41.960
A square needs two matching groups of prime tiles.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.000
Thirty-six splits into two sixes.

00:01:45.330 --> 00:01:47.980
Forty-eight has four twos and one three.

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:49.720
The three has no partner.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.410
Multiply by another three and the tiles

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:53.460
can split evenly.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:55.350
The new total is one hundred and forty-four,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.340
or twelve squared.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.590
Use the smaller prime counts for a

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:01.960
highest common factor.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.960
Use the larger counts for a lowest common multiple.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.760
A square has even counts of every prime.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.990
In your notebook, find the highest common factor and

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:13.480
lowest common multiple of twenty and thirty.
